<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ziamet">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziamet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th–16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as zeamet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ziamet was a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> between the 14th and 16th centuries: a grant of lands or revenues by the sultan to an individual in return for services, especially military. The holder was an agent of the central government, supervising the possession, transfer and rental of lands in his territory and collecting tax revenue. The system was introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osman-i">Osman I</a>, who granted land tenure to his troops, and expanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a> for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sipahi">Sipahi</a> cavalry; an institution taken over from the Seljuq sultanate. As central control weakened in the 16th and 17th centuries, ziamets were enlarged, given to fewer holders for longer tenures and converted into tax farms or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a>.</p></div>
